# API organization (Kanban Board API)

## Route layout

- `src/api/public/` → `/public/...` — register, login, health check (no auth required)
- `src/api/user/` → `/user/...` — boards, columns, tasks, board-member invites (authenticated user routes)

Session/auth helpers live under `src/api/public/auth/` — `POST /public/auth/register`, `POST /public/auth/login`, `POST /public/auth/refresh`. These stay public; do not gate them behind `@BoardAuth()`.

Global Nest prefix is `api/v1`, so full paths are `/api/v1/public/...`, `/api/v1/user/...`.

## Request flow (strict — no skipping layers)

Every API request follows this chain. Each layer only talks to the one directly below it:

```
Controller (src/api/…)  →  Service  →  Repository  →  Prisma table
```

| Step | Layer | Location | Calls | Never calls |
|------|-------|----------|-------|-------------|
| 1 | **Controller** | `src/api/<area>/<feature>/` | Feature service | Repository, Prisma |
| 2 | **Service** | `src/api/<area>/<feature>/` | Repository service(s) | Prisma / `PrismaService` for entity CRUD |
| 3 | **Repository** | `src/repositories/<entity>-repository/` | `PrismaService` → model delegate (`user`, `board`, …) | Controllers, feature services |
| 4 | **Prisma table** | PostgreSQL via `@prisma/client` | — | — |

```typescript
// Controller — HTTP only, delegates to service
@Post()
create(@Body({ schema: createBoardSchema }) body: CreateBoardDto) {
  return this.boardService.create(body);
}

// Service — business logic, auth checks, transactions; delegates to repository
async create(dto: CreateBoardDto) {
  return this.boardRepository.create({ name: dto.name });
}

// Repository — single-entity Prisma access only
create(data: Prisma.BoardCreateInput, tx?: Prisma.TransactionClient) {
  const client = tx ?? this.prisma;
  return client.board.create({ data });
}
```

Do not put business logic in controllers. Do not put HTTP concerns in repositories. Do not query Prisma tables from feature services.

## Access control

- `@BoardAuth()` — class or method-level guard, checks the requesting user has a `BoardMember` row for the `:boardId` in the route param before allowing the request through. Applies to every controller under `board/`, `column/`, `task/`.
- Guard lives in `src/api/user/auth/guards/board-auth.guard.ts`
- Import `BoardAuthModule` in any feature module whose controller uses `@BoardAuth()`
- Role checks (`OWNER` / `EDITOR` / `VIEWER`) happen inside the guard or a follow-up `@Roles(...)` decorator — never inline in the controller/service

## Repository pattern (required for all DB access)

All database reads and writes go through `src/repositories/`. **Never** call `PrismaService` (or `prisma.*`) directly from API controllers or feature services.

### Folder layout

One folder per Prisma model / aggregate, kebab-case with a `-repository` suffix:

```
src/repositories/
  user-repository/
    user-repository.module.ts
    user-repository.service.ts
  board-repository/
    board-repository.module.ts
    board-repository.service.ts
  board-member-repository/
    board-member-repository.module.ts
    board-member-repository.service.ts
  column-repository/
    column-repository.module.ts
    column-repository.service.ts
  task-repository/
    task-repository.module.ts
    task-repository.service.ts
```

Each repository folder exports exactly two files — a Nest module and a service. No controllers, DTOs, or guards inside `repositories/`.

### Naming

| Item | Pattern | Example |
|------|---------|---------|
| Folder | `<entity>-repository/` | `board-member-repository/` |
| Module file | `<entity>-repository.module.ts` | `board-repository.module.ts` |
| Service file | `<entity>-repository.service.ts` | `board-repository.service.ts` |
| Module class | `<Entity>RepositoryModule` | `BoardRepositoryModule` |
| Service class | `<Entity>RepositoryService` | `BoardRepositoryService` |

### Service implementation

- Inject `PrismaService` in the constructor — **not** `PrismaClient` directly.
- One repository service owns all Prisma access for that entity (find, create, update, delete, scoped lists).
- Methods return Prisma model types or `null`; no HTTP exceptions inside repositories.
- Accept an optional `tx` (`Prisma.TransactionClient`) on methods that may run inside a transaction:

```typescript
@Injectable()
export class UserRepositoryService {
  constructor(private readonly prisma: PrismaService) {}

  findByEmail(email: string, tx?: Prisma.TransactionClient) {
    const client = tx ?? this.prisma;
    return client.user.findUnique({ where: { email } });
  }

  findById(id: string, tx?: Prisma.TransactionClient) {
    const client = tx ?? this.prisma;
    return client.user.findUnique({ where: { id } });
  }

  create(data: Prisma.UserCreateInput, tx?: Prisma.TransactionClient) {
    const client = tx ?? this.prisma;
    return client.user.create({ data });
  }
}
```

### Module wiring

- Each `*-repository.module.ts` imports `PrismaModule` and exports its repository service.
- Feature modules (`src/api/...`) import the repository module(s) they need — never `PrismaModule` directly unless the feature service orchestrates a transaction via `PrismaService.transaction()`.

```typescript
@Module({
  imports: [PrismaModule],
  providers: [BoardRepositoryService],
  exports: [BoardRepositoryService],
})
export class BoardRepositoryModule {}
```

### Layer responsibilities

| Layer | Location | Responsibility |
|-------|----------|----------------|
| Controller | `src/api/<area>/<feature>/` | Routes, DTO binding, guards — **no DB** |
| Service | `src/api/<area>/<feature>/` | Business rules, orchestration, `$transaction` — **no direct Prisma** |
| Repository | `src/repositories/<entity>-repository/` | CRUD and queries for one entity — **Prisma only** |
| Prisma table | `users`, `boards`, `board_members`, … | Persistence |

- Feature services orchestrate multi-entity flows and pass `tx` into repository calls inside `$transaction`.
- Locking helpers (`advisoryXactLock`, row locks) live in `src/common/db/` — see [database-locking](database-locking.mdc).
- `PrismaService` (`src/prisma/`) is infrastructure: connection, `$transaction`, health check — not a substitute for repositories.

## Feature layout (API)

```
src/api/user/board/
  board.controller.ts
  board.service.ts
  board.module.ts
  dto/
    create-board.dto.ts
    update-board.dto.ts
    board-response.dto.ts
```

- Put Zod schemas in `dto/` — no loose `*.schemas.ts` beside controllers.
- Wire features via area modules (`PublicApiModule`, `UserApiModule`) into `AppModule`.

## Rules

1. Controllers and feature services **never** import `@prisma/client` or call `PrismaService` for entity CRUD — use repository services.
2. One repository folder per entity; do not combine unrelated models in one repository service.
3. Place controllers/services/modules/DTOs under the matching `src/api/<area>/` folder.
4. Controller prefixes must match the folder (`public`, `user`).
5. Authenticated board routes use `@BoardAuth()`; modules import `BoardAuthModule`.
6. **Race-safe writes** — multi-step flows use `$transaction` + locks — see [database-locking](database-locking.mdc).

## Checklist for new endpoints

- [ ] Chose `public` or `user` correctly
- [ ] Files under `src/api/<area>/...` with `dto/` for inputs/docs
- [ ] New entity DB access added in `src/repositories/<entity>-repository/`, not in the feature service
- [ ] Repository module imported in the feature module
- [ ] Auth: public routes vs `@BoardAuth()` (class or method) as appropriate
- [ ] `@Controller('<area>/...')` matches the folder
- [ ] Module wired into the area module / `AppModule`
- [ ] Contested writes use tx + correct lock (advisory vs row lock)
